<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Northern Cyprus</h1><p class="st-meta">1983 CE – present · capital North Nicosia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A de facto state in the northern third of Cyprus, declared in 1983 and recognised only by Turkey; others call it territory of the Republic of Cyprus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Turkish Republic</a> of Northern Cyprus declared independence on 15 November 1983, in the part of the island that Turkish forces took in 1974 after a coup backed by the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> against President Makarios. UN Security Council Resolution 541 rejected the declaration as legally invalid. Only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> recognises it; the Republic of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, the EU and the UN regard the area as Cypriot territory under Turkish military occupation.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The north took shape after intercommunal violence in 1963 and 1964 drove about 20,000 Turkish Cypriots into enclaves, and after the 1975 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> agreement under which 196,000 Greek Cypriots in the north were exchanged for 42,000 Turkish Cypriots in the south. Rauf Denktaş led the Turkish Cypriot side. It is a semi-presidential republic with a 50-member Assembly. It depends on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> for economic, political and military support; some experts call it a puppet state of Turkey, while others say the label does not fit.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>In the 2004 referendum on the Annan Plan, 65% of Turkish Cypriots accepted the proposal, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> then suspended its law in the north. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Mehmet Ali</a> Talat succeeded Denktaş, and Derviş Eroğlu won the presidency in 2010. Ersin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> was elected the fifth president in October 2020 against Mustafa Akıncı, and Tufan Erhürman later became president, with Ünal Üstel as prime minister.</p></div><aside 